-# Native Avalanche recovery and clean-stop regression
-
-The development node completes existing pending runtime journal, receipt and
-invalidity propagation work before constructing the pool, Avalanche driver,
-listener or ready event. Startup uses the existing bounded `DevelopmentRuntime`
-Tick rules. It stops as soon as the runtime is idle and never runs candidate
-selection from an idle snapshot. Paused execution, missing authority, unresolved
-work and operational failures refuse startup while preserving durable state.
-Startup permits at most 10,000 Tick calls and 120 seconds measured by a monotonic
-clock. The deadline is checked between bounded Tick calls, including before
-reporting ready; it cannot interrupt a Tick already in progress.
-
-AvalancheDriver::Save persists the last active admitted public peer cache even
-when a runtime journal makes chain authority temporarily unavailable. Saving
-this cache does not assert current eligibility, quorum readiness or finality.
-Start still individually screens restored records against the recovered native
-chain, then atomically restores the valid peer metadata. Real PeerStore failures
-remain fatal and revoke driver readiness. No private session keys are saved.
-
-The focused executable links the actual runtime, driver and peer store. CMake
-extracts the exact startup recovery function from the node command into a test
-include; the test does not maintain a separate model of the recovery policy.
-It creates a real native pending journal from an authenticated fixture block,
-verifies that live driver activity and direct Start remain gated, saves the
-public cache during pending work, and completes recovery before Start. It also
-closes and reopens the actual pending database, verifies the expected recovered
-tip, checks both initial and final-Tick deadline refusal, confirms idle startup
-performs no selection, and confirms a real cache-write failure remains fatal.
-The focused save test uses an observer with no admitted proof coins; retention
-of funded proofs and real nine-peer node lifecycle are separate acceptance
-checks against copies of the native 2024-block fixture.
-
-```sh
-cmake -S src/lotus_v2/avalanche_lifecycle_test \
-  -B build-avalanche-lifecycle-review -G Ninja \
-  -DLOTUS_ABC_BUILD=/private/tmp/lotus-v2-daemon-runtime-staging-20261015/build \
-  -DLOTUS_SECP_LIBRARY=/private/tmp/lotus-v2-daemon-runtime-staging-20261015/build/src/secp256k1/libsecp256k1.a \
-  -DLOTUS_BOOST_INCLUDE=/opt/homebrew/include
