-# Development peer dispatcher integration tests
-
-This target connects two real V4 `DevelopmentRuntime` owners through the actual
-`Session`, `Transport`, shared `DownloadManager` and `PeerDispatcher`. Most cases
-use bounded memory byte streams with short reads/writes; the final case uses the
-POSIX TCP adapter on explicitly requested `127.0.0.1:0`. The test checks the
-allocated port excludes 10604, 10605 and 8331. It starts no daemon and reads no
-existing data directory.
-
-The immutable complete-block fork corpus and exact validator profiles in
-`fixtures.h` come from the existing recovery tests at `f93ea73fd`, through the
-runtime test fixture header. Its provenance comments and golden wire, UTXO and
-monetary bytes are retained. ChainID, genesis, profile binding, storage options,
-recovery/selection limits and peer limits are explicit test inputs. The memory
-streams implement byte movement only; chain admission, ordinary validation,
-selection, journaling, restoration and invalidity use the real implementation.
-These historical fixture profiles are not a network profile adoption.
-
-## Build and run
-
-The source tree must include the runtime, dispatcher and TCP implementation
-packets. This test commit contains only this directory and
-`test_peer_dispatcher.cpp`; it does not contain borrowed implementation sources.
-Using a separate native worktree with those dependencies integrated:
-
-```sh
-cmake -S src/lotus_v2/peer_dispatcher_test -B build-peer-dispatcher -GNinja \
-  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
-  -DLOTUS_SECP_LIBRARY=/path/to/ABC/libsecp256k1.a
-cmake --build build-peer-dispatcher --target peer_dispatcher_test -j4
-ctest --test-dir build-peer-dispatcher -R '^peer_dispatcher_test$' --output-on-failure
-
-cmake -S src/lotus_v2/peer_dispatcher_test -B build-peer-dispatcher-sanitize -GNinja \
-  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug -DLOTUS_SANITIZE=ON \
-  -DLOTUS_SECP_LIBRARY=/path/to/ABC/libsecp256k1.a
-cmake --build build-peer-dispatcher-sanitize --target peer_dispatcher_test -j4
-ctest --test-dir build-peer-dispatcher-sanitize -R '^peer_dispatcher_test$' --output-on-failure
-```
-
-The isolated target builds bundled LevelDB/crc32c, V2 validation and peering
-sources directly, and links OpenSSL and the supplied ABC secp256k1 library. Boost
-headers are found by the existing disk-state test configuration. TCP is currently
-supported on Linux/macOS; the observed run was macOS arm64. Sanitizer mode covers
-the locally compiled sources and bundled storage libraries; the supplied secp
-archive has its own build provenance/instrumentation.
-
-## Checks
-
-- Five complete blocks sync to exact reference tip, root, header, monetary bytes,
-  touched CoinEntries and authenticated coin proofs. Header pages contain at most
-  two entries and reach height five before the receiver advances its active tip.
-- Compact downloads exchange missing ordinary transactions with an empty cache.
-  A separate one-transaction reconstruction limit forces native full-block
-  fallback. Download retention never claims contextual chain acceptance.
-- Five concurrently reserved full-body jobs share one session. Gating the remote
-  after its first full body allows an ordinary active-tip advance while four
-  jobs and their staging leases remain live; all complete without reconnect or
-  re-request. Runtime context changes do not change the fetch epoch.
-- A real 32-frame Transport queue saturates while writes block. A separately
-  pending dispatcher response times out, closes the connection, releases jobs
-  and leaves permanent invalidity empty. Usage counters respect configured caps.
-- A LevelDB WAL wrapper reports failure after an actual successful durable Sync.
-  Runtime authority disappears, the dispatcher fences/cancels pending fetches,
-  and explicit runtime reopen reconciles the authoritative store. The storage
-  seam supplies no consensus decisions.
-- A remote sends header-authenticated but contextually invalid branch bodies
-  through the actual transport. Ordinary validation rejects the invalid middle,
-  journal recovery restores exact source state, and durable descendant exclusion
-  preserves its causal root and advances the fetch fence.
-- Repeated one-action dispatcher ticks release an expired download lease. With
-  zero transport allowance, buffered application work for two peers sharing one
-  manager still progresses fairly. Events are primed through the dispatcher so
-  its event-ready observations remain consistent with the borrowed transports.
-- Actual ephemeral localhost TCP repeats the five-block full-sync checkpoint on
-  one connection between two runtimes in the same process.
-
-Fresh temporary homes are removed after success. On exception, their paths are
-printed and preserved for inspection. The before-fix one-action timeout failure
-is retained in `/private/tmp/lotus-v2-peer-dispatcher-before-fairness.log`; its
-failed homes are also preserved. These tests establish same-process runtime
-integration, not two daemon processes, public peer discovery, mainnet behavior,
-production resource calibration or an adopted V2 network.
-
-## Observed isolated run
