-## Network audit regressions (2026-10-15)
-
-At native base `63c51499441ded6d0fe34d87ff43d53a14b1398f`, ordinary
-active-tip changes already preserve fetch epochs. The reported tip-advance
-invalidation is stale; the existing concurrent-body test covers that behavior.
-Actual authority loss and durable invalidity changes still advance the fetch
-epoch and cancel stale downloads.
-
-The added authority-loss regression transfers one inbound application into the
-dispatcher's input slot under a one-action budget, commits one output, then
-causes a real ambiguous LevelDB WAL sync result. The old code fails
-`authority fence retains application connection`. The repair retains the
-original bounded bytes and output deadline, pauses input/output while authority
-is absent, and resumes each application exactly once after explicit reopen.
-Cases cover tx, avahello, inv, getdata and notfound. A mixed tx/block notfound
-case emits its tx half before the fence, retires its rewritten block-only half,
-and verifies a later fresh tx notfound still reaches the application consumer.
-The existing invalid-fork recovery test additionally retains one committed
-application output across the actual invalidity-epoch change.
-
-The test target passes all 13 reported groups after repair, including retained
-fetch leases, cancellation, bounded notices/output, deadline scheduling,
-recovery and actual ephemeral TCP synchronization. These application tests
-verify dispatcher ownership and byte delivery; they do not claim Avalanche
-proof/poll validation or node-level mixed-inventory retry coverage.
-
-The reported mixed tx/proof inventory rejection is stale at the enclosing node:
-`Node::HandleApplication` in `development_node_command.cpp` partitions the
-application notice into transaction and proof messages, admits the transaction
-half once, and retains only the proof half on Avalanche backpressure. This
-source inspection does not supply a live node regression for that retry edge.
-No second partition implementation is added to the dispatcher.
-
-Fixture homes now use `std::filesystem::temp_directory_path` and `mkdtemp`;
-there is no requirement for the Darwin-specific `/private/tmp` path.
