--- a/src/lotus_v2/peer_session_test/README.md
+++ /dev/null
@@ -1,135 +0,0 @@
-# Socket-free V2 handshake/session candidate
-
-This packet adds the bounded inherited ABC version codec and a single-owner
-V2 session state machine on top of `peer_codec`. It opens no sockets, selects no
-network/profile and changes no daemon dispatcher, mainnet parameters or shared
-build files. All identities, magic, transaction profile, receive limits, nonce,
-entropy and monotonic timeout are caller-supplied. Local version service fields
-must all be zero: activation makes no history-serving or validation promise.
-Test network identities/magic are arbitrary literals, not allocations.
-
-## Inherited version behavior
-
-`DecodeVersion` requires version >=70017 and at most the independent pre-handshake
-2 MiB envelope capacity. It reproduces the inspected ABC `net_processing.cpp`
-version parser, rather than imposing an invented exact modern payload size:
-
-| Component | Bytes / behavior |
-| --- | --- |
-| Required prefix | version:i32, services:u64, time:i64, receiver services:u64, raw address[16], port:u16 big-endian: 46 bytes |
-| Optional sender group | sender services:u64, raw address[16], port:u16 big-endian, nonce:u64: 34 bytes |
-| Optional user agent | Shortest CompactSize plus 0..256 raw bytes |
-| Optional starting height | i32 |
-| Optional relay | One byte; any nonzero value means true |
-| Optional extra entropy | u64 |
-| Unknown trailing suffix | Ignored after entropy, bounded by the envelope; only its length is retained |
-
-Each optional group is parsed if bytes remain. A truncated present group rejects;
-ending immediately before a group accepts. Missing sender nonce and entropy
-have accessor defaults 1, starting height -1, relay true and no user agent.
-Negative advertised timestamp normalizes to zero. Fields other than ports are
-little-endian. Version addresses do not have CAddress timestamps. User-agent
-bytes remain untrusted diagnostics; caller sanitizes logging independently.
-
-The complete modern form emitted by a session has 94..352 bytes. This is not a
-new strict receive bound: a peer may send an inherited optional prefix or bounded
-unknown trailing extension. `EncodeVersion` emits known contiguous suffix fields
-only; `ignored_suffix_bytes` is diagnostic, not an encoder extension field. Its
-bool-returning encode/decode functions preserve the result on failure.
-
-Inbound sessions invoke the injected nonce predicate once for the first accepted
-version. As upstream does, an omitted sender/nonce group still passes sentinel 1
-to that predicate. A false result rejects a self connection; an exception is an
-operational local failure. This mechanism does not authenticate peer identity.
-Outbound sessions do not invoke that inbound check. Duplicate version is ignored
-without parsing, replacing state, rechecking nonce or extending a deadline.
-
-## Session API and ownership
-
-Call `OnOpen(monotonic_ms)` once, then pass fragmented/coalesced TCP bytes through
-`Feed(bytes,monotonic_ms,consumed)` and call `OnTimer(monotonic_ms)` independently.
-Time is injected monotonic milliseconds; timeout is explicit and positive.
-Deadline addition is checked for overflow. Backward time is a local failure.
-Handshake expiry is inclusive at the fixed deadline; traffic never extends it.
-An active session no longer has a handshake deadline.
-
-Each call emits at most one `SessionEvent`. `Feed` consumes at most one complete
-envelope, so the caller must re-feed the remaining span. An event carries at
-most two ordered, complete outbound handshake frames. The caller atomically
-queues all outbound frames into its bounded send queue or closes the session;
-returned frames count as queued/sent for choreography. Calls must be serialized.
-The session is not copyable. It owns no output queue or connection.
-
-Both endpoint roles emit complete version first. Receiving the first acceptable
-version emits hello and optionally sendaddrv2. Accepting peer hello emits verack.
-Only receiving peer verack after accepted hello activates the session, after
-both local hello/verack actions have been emitted. Activation switches the
-embedded envelope decoder to min(local,remote) capacity while between messages,
-before any following coalesced application frame is read. Before activation,
-version/hello retain the independent 2 MiB / 118-byte bounds even when advertised
-capacity is 1..117 bytes. Limit changes require a new session/connection.
-
-Duplicate hello, premature verack, pre-version commands and pre-activation
-application commands reject. Duplicate accepted version/verack are ignored as
-upstream does. Sendaddrv2 records support only; it starts no address relay and
-is permitted after version before verack, as the V2 proposal requires.
-
-One existing candidate policy differs from ABC handler permissiveness:
-`peer_codec` requires empty verack/sendaddrv2 payloads, while ABC's handlers
-ignore unused payload bytes. This session inherits that strict empty-control
-codec bound and tests checksum-valid nonempty frames rejecting at their header.
-It does not silently claim legacy parity for those control payloads. Likewise,
-the V2 hello sequence and >=70017 minimum intentionally supersede legacy ABC
-handshake sequencing/minimum. Root protocol review must record these choices.
-
-After activation, only N02/N03/N04 application commands are dispatched as opaque,
-unvalidated messages. Unknown optional commands, feefilter, Bloom/filter,
-Avalanche, snapshots, package and mempool enumeration commands are ignored by
-this layer; it has no inherited handlers for them. Session activation and an
-application event do not validate a transaction/header/body, advertise network
-history, request high-bandwidth compact announcements or mutate chainstate.
-
-`PeerRejected`, `LocalFailure`, `TimedOut` and `Closed` remain distinct outcomes.
-None implies a consensus-invalid block or TxID. `OnClosed` releases partial input
-and peer version state. Allocation failures remain operational exceptions: the
-caller closes/cleans up rather than penalizing consensus objects. The embedded
-decoder can buffer one bounded message; the caller still bounds the number of
-sessions, per-peer/global bytes, event processing, send queues and pending work.
-No transaction/block/address download allocation or parsing occurs here. A
-pre-activation forbidden command's envelope may be buffered up to the explicit
-pre-handshake cap before rejection; global connection budgets remain required.
-
-## Remaining runtime work
-
-The packet has no socket/listener, datadir/profile loader, network adoption,
-address discovery, RPC, validated-body availability index, service upgrade,
-headers synchronization, in-flight downloads, mempool, compact scheduling,
-reconstruction lifecycle/tombstone owner, fallback/retry loop, chain admission,
-fork choice, persistent connection/reorg or public-network claim. The runtime
-must retain and bound per-peer/global compact tombstones until disconnect as
-required by the separate codec packet. A zero-service session is useful for
-isolated handshake tests; it is not a claim of a usable synchronization node.
-
-## Verification
-
-```sh
-cmake -S src/lotus_v2/peer_session_test -B /tmp/v2-peer-session-build -GNinja \
-  -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug \
-  -DLOTUS_SECP_LIBRARY=/absolute/path/to/libsecp256k1.a
-cmake --build /tmp/v2-peer-session-build
-ctest --test-dir /tmp/v2-peer-session-build --output-on-failure
-```
-
-Set `-DLOTUS_SANITIZE=ON` for AddressSanitizer/UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.
-No synthetic hashing seam or Chronik is enabled. The target reuses native V2
-codec sources and inherited SHA256/SipHash plus an existing ABC secp archive.
-
-Coverage includes a literal independent version byte vector and big-endian
-ports, every truncated optional suffix, 252/253/256 user-agent boundaries,
-noncanonical lengths, relay values, negative time, omitted nonce sentinel,
-unknown suffix up to exact 2 MiB, duplicate version/verack versus hello,
-identity/features/self nonce, local services refusal, deadline/no extension,
-clock overflow/backward movement, premature application messages, split reads,
-limits 1/117/118 with coalesced activation then oversized application rejection,
-asymmetric capacities, unsupported command isolation, empty control records,
-and two socket-free sessions exchanging their actual handshake actions.
