-# Bounded transaction relay review
-
-This engine routes negotiated V2 `inv`, `getdata`, `notfound` inventory type 1
-(TxHash) and canonical `tx` bodies through existing Session envelopes. Feature
-bit 8 denotes transaction relay; bit 16 remains reserved for Avalanche. Set
-SessionConfig supported_features to mandatory|8 and required_features to
-mandatory|8 where transaction relay is required. Peers without common bit 8
-cannot send `tx`. Inventory types for blocks retain their existing meanings;
-the dispatcher must route or split shared inventory commands by type.
-
-`AddPeer` requires activated session limit and authorization. Feed application
-messages to `Handle`, and call `Tick` with monotonic milliseconds. Only an
-outstanding same-peer TxHash authorizes body admission. The engine checks exact
-canonical reserialization and derives TxHash with the real identity codec before
-calling the authoritative pool callback. Only Accepted results announce;
-Duplicate and Conflict do not punish a peer. Invalid is peer-invalid; capacity,
-stale context, storage failures and callback exceptions are local failures.
-The Read callback must return only accepted canonical pool transactions and
-must honor the pool's authoritative context fence. Admission callbacks receive
-one body per invocation and must apply their own explicit VM/work budget.
-
-All output actions must be staged atomically into bounded Transport output, or
-the caller must RemovePeer for any action it cannot stage. This removes that
-peer's outstanding requests; already accepted pool transactions remain valid.
-The engine holds no output payload queue between calls. Each call caps action
-count and aggregate framed bytes; timers retry issued requests within a fixed
-retry bound. Dispatcher processing must also have its existing per-tick ingress
-budget: per-call bounds do not imply unlimited calls per tick are safe.
-
-Missing-parent bodies occupy a separate bounded byte/count/expiry queue. Tick
-and accepted arrivals retry admission with a shared per-call validation count;
-retry exhaustion/expiry silently drops local retained bodies. Parent discovery
-uses the sender's bounded parent-before-child pool inventory pages, supplied
-through AnnounceMany for initial join and new accepted ancestors. A TxID from an
-input is never interpreted as a TxHash, and there is no invented conversion or
-ancestor wire extension. Callers must page the pool snapshot across calls and
-retry pages after local output refusal. Missing-parent recovery requires an
-ancestor announcement or independent prior pool knowledge. This is explicit
-protocol behavior, not a guarantee that arbitrary unavailable ancestors can be
-fetched.
-
-RelayLimits are local private-test configuration defaults, not mainnet policy:
-16 peers, 1024 known hashes per peer (16384 globally), 16 requests per peer/128
-globally, 128 inventory entries per message, 32 actions/1 MiB framed action
-bytes per call, 256 KiB transaction bodies, 8 validation attempts per call,
-32 missing-parent bodies/1 MiB total, 5 second request timeout, 30 second orphan
-expiry and 2 retries. Known inventories use a bounded first-seen FIFO with a
-set for membership; duplicate sightings do not refresh eviction order. High
-hash values cannot pin older entries ahead of newer low hashes. Entries can
-be evicted; duplicate admission never re-announces. No unsolicited transaction
-body is retained. The existing Session decoder caps frame allocation separately.
-
-Build with the local ABC secp library:
-
-```sh
-cmake -S src/lotus_v2/transaction_relay_test -B /tmp/relay-review \
-  -DLOTUS_SECP_LIBRARY=/absolute/path/libsecp256k1.a
-cmake --build /tmp/relay-review
-ctest --test-dir /tmp/relay-review --output-on-failure
-```
-
-When transaction_pool.h is present, the target also tests actual pool/VM
-admission and child-before-parent recovery. LOTUS_POOL_SOURCE can point to the
-separately reviewed pool directory; LOTUS_BOOST_INCLUDE selects local Boost.
-The test always exercises real codec, actual TxHash and byte-split Session
-framing. It covers outstanding matching, malformed transaction, duplicates,
-timeouts, disconnect, capability denial and local backpressure. These are
-standalone API checks, not TCP socket or multi-node network acceptance evidence.
-
-Audit regression at native base `63c51499441ded6d0fe34d87ff43d53a14b1398f`:
-with a two-entry known cache, sight `ff...`, `fe...`, then `01...`. The old
-lexicographic eviction fails the assertion that announcing `ff...` sends one
-action; the FIFO repair passes it and the existing real pool/VM tests. This
-checks cache suppression/eviction, without making a throughput claim.
